        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D1 [verdict vs criteriaAssessments C4 / CL12 / correctedUserAssumptions[2]] C4, CL12, correctedUserAssumptions[2] and practicalImplications[3] now say the 48-month winner 'cannot be called either way with confidence', because a lower borrowing rate and a risk-adjusted residual rate pull in opposite directions. The verdict text was not changed, and it still presents the discount-rate flip in one direction only ('It reverses ... if the right discount rate is below about 7.53% per year'). So the headline and the revised body now disagree on how the 48-month result responds to the discount rate. This does not change the 72-month recommendation."
        },
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D2 [criteriaAssessments C4 assessment and weaknesses; CL12; remainingUncertainty[4]] The new risk-adjusted-residual counterpoint is left purely qualitative ('net effect is not quantified'), but a one-line illustration is easy and would help the CFO. For example, discounting the $90k residual at 12%/12 gives v48 of about 0.6203, so the residual's PV falls from about $65.4k to about $55.8k and the lease's 48-month edge grows by about $9.6k. That is about twice the base gap. Without a number, the reader cannot tell whether this effect outweighs the borrowing-rate effect it is set against."
        },
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D3 [alternatives[3] 'Lease 48 months, then buy a successor at month 48'] The revised 'Lease 48 months, then buy a successor' text mixes valuation bases. It gives the threshold as '$70,381 PV ... or about $96,821 paid at month 48, plus any resale credit'. The resale credit is a month-72 amount, so adding it to a month-48 nominal figure needs it converted to a month-48 value. The text also credits the successor's year-6 resale without netting the year-6 resale the buy-and-keep path would get for the old unit (K16 assumes R = 0 for buy-and-keep). The extra credit should be the difference between the two resale values, not the successor's whole resale."
        }
      ]
    },
